Question 1: Without a volume, everything written to /mlflow-data stays in the container's own writable layer. If I stop the container and start the same one again, the data is still there. But when I removed it with docker rm and started a new container from the same image, the mlflow UI was empty and my test-persistence experiment was gone. Every new container starts from the clean image.

Question 2: A bind mount would work, but a named volume is a better choice. It doesn't depend on a folder path on my own computer, so the compose file works on any machine. Docker manages it, so there are no permission problems, and on Windows it is faster and avoids SQLite locking issues. The database also can't end up in git by mistake, and Compose controls when it is kept or deleted.

Question 3: Docker Compose creates a private network for the project and puts all the services on it. Docker's DNS on that network turns each service name into the container's IP, so "mlflow" points to the mlflow container. In Lab 3, mlflow was running on my computer and not in a container, and the API container was on the default network, which has no name resolution. That is why I needed host.docker.internal or --network host.

Question 4: The name "inference" only exists inside the Compose network. If I run the frontend image alone with docker run, http://inference:8000 won't resolve and every upload will fail. With an environment variable, the same image works everywhere without rebuilding. I just pass a different URL depending on where it runs. This keeps the configuration separate from the code.

Question 5: No person needs to call the inference API directly. Only the frontend uses it, and containers on the same Compose network can reach each other without publishing ports. The Streamlit code runs inside the frontend container, so my browser only talks to port 8501 and the frontend container is the one calling http://inference:8000. Not publishing the port is also safer and avoids port conflicts.

Question 6: My serve.py catches the error at startup. It prints "Error loading model", leaves the model empty and starts the API anyway. The container keeps running and /health still works, but every /predict returns 503 "Model is not loaded" and the frontend shows that error. I saw this in docker compose logs inference the first time I started the stack, because the registry was still empty. If the error was not caught, the container would just exit. To fix it, I can add a healthcheck on mlflow with condition service_healthy, add a retry in serve.py, use restart on-failure, or run docker compose restart inference.

Question 7: docker compose ps shows 0.0.0.0:5000->5000/tcp for mlflow and 0.0.0.0:8501->8501/tcp for frontend. Inference only shows 8000/tcp, which means the port is open inside the network but not mapped to my computer. This matches the compose file, where only mlflow and frontend have a ports section.

Question 8: The prediction still came from the old model version. The same image gave the same confidence. serve.py loads models:/food11@champion only once at startup and keeps it in memory, so moving the alias changes nothing until the container restarts. The command is docker compose restart inference. After running it, the same image gave a different confidence because version 2 was loaded.

Question 9: The inference image only contains the code and the Python libraries, not the model. Every time the container starts, it asks mlflow which version champion points to and downloads it. A restart runs this startup step again in the same container, so it picks up the new version without rebuilding anything.

Question 10: After down and up, everything was still there: the registered model, its versions and the champion alias. down removes the containers and the network but keeps named volumes, so the database and artifacts on mlflow-data were safe. After down -v, the volume was deleted too. mlflow started empty, inference couldn't load food11@champion, and the frontend showed 503 until I retrained, registered the model again and restarted inference.

Question 11: Docker Compose only runs containers on one machine. It can't spread containers over several machines, move them when a machine fails, or balance traffic between hosts. For three inference replicas behind a load balancer, I would need an orchestrator like Kubernetes, with a Deployment of 3 replicas and a Service to balance the traffic. For mlflow to survive a machine failure, I would replace the SQLite file with an external database like PostgreSQL and store the artifacts in object storage like S3 or MinIO. Then mlflow itself holds no data and can run on several machines. The images would also need to be pushed to a registry so other machines can download them.
